::ALG1_U01_PT_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
In the expression 7x + 4, what is the coefficient?
{
=A. 7#Correct. The coefficient is the number multiplying the variable x.
~B. x#This is the variable, not the coefficient. The coefficient is the numerical factor.
~C. 4#This is the constant term. It does not multiply the variable.
~D. 11#This is the sum of 7 and 4, not a named part of the expression.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Evaluate 2x + 3 when x = 5.
{
~A. 10#This evaluates 2x but leaves out the +3. Substitute first, then complete the whole expression.
~B. 25#This multiplies 2 and 5, then attaches the 3 incorrectly. Follow order of operations.
=C. 13#Correct. Substitute 5 for x: 2(5) + 3 = 10 + 3 = 13.
~D. 53#This places 5 and 3 next to each other instead of adding after multiplication.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Evaluate -6 + 14.
{
~A. -20#This adds the absolute values and keeps the negative sign. The positive value has greater absolute value.
=B. 8#Correct. Moving 14 units right from -6 lands at 8.
~C. -8#This has the sign reversed. The result is positive because 14 is farther from zero than -6.
~D. 20#This adds the absolute values but ignores the sign relationship.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.4
Question:
Which value is equivalent to 0.4?
{
~A. 4%#This moves the decimal one place too few. 0.4 is 40%.
~B. 400%#This moves the decimal one place too many.
~C. 0.04%#This makes the value much smaller than 0.4.
=D. 40%#Correct. 0.4 multiplied by 100 equals 40%.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 3².
{
~A. 6#This multiplies 3 by 2. An exponent means repeated multiplication.
=B. 9#Correct. 3² = 3 times 3 = 9.
~C. 5#This adds the base and exponent. Exponents do not mean addition.
~D. 8#This uses 2³ instead of 3².
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 16⁽¹⁄²⁾.
{
=A. 4#Correct. 16⁽¹⁄²⁾ is the square root of 16, which is 4.
~B. 8#This divides 16 by 2. An exponent of 1/2 means square root.
~C. 32#This multiplies 16 by 2 instead of finding the square root.
~D. 256#This squares 16 instead of finding the square root.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 2³ times 2².
{
~A. 12#This multiplies visible numbers incorrectly. Use exponent rules or repeated multiplication.
~B. 64#This multiplies exponents to get too large a power. Add exponents when multiplying powers with the same base.
=C. 32#Correct. 2³ times 2² = 2⁵ = 32.
~D. 10#This adds 8 and 2 or combines values incorrectly.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite x⁴ times x³.
{
~A. x¹²#This multiplies the exponents. For product of powers with the same base, add the exponents.
~B. 2x⁷#This adds an extra coefficient. There is no coefficient 2 in the expression.
~C. x#This subtracts exponents instead of adding them.
=D. x⁷#Correct. x⁴ times x³ = x⁽⁴⁺³⁾ = x⁷.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Simplify 5a + 3a.
{
=A. 8a#Correct. 5a and 3a are like terms, so 5a + 3a = 8a.
~B. 15a#This multiplies the coefficients. Like terms are combined by adding coefficients.
~C. 8a²#This changes the variable part. Combining like terms keeps the variable part the same.
~D. 2a#This subtracts the coefficients instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_PT_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite 3(x + 4) using the distributive property.
{
~A. 3x + 4#This multiplies 3 by x but not by 4. Distribute to every term inside the parentheses.
=B. 3x + 12#Correct. Multiply 3 by x and multiply 3 by 4.
~C. 7x#This adds 3 and 4 and attaches x. The distributive property uses multiplication.
~D. 12x#This multiplies 3 and 4 but does not include the x-term correctly.
}
